{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>ואתה תחזה.</b> Meaning, not that the people would appoint them, but he himself would see. Further, he alluded that on this account he is considered as though he himself is the judge, since they are his emissaries, and he takes a share in the mitzvah." "<b>מכל העם.</b> Meaning, even if he finds people who appear in his eyes worthy for the matter, nevertheless he should not be satisfied with them until he searches through all the people and selects the greatest among them in every respect, from among the good qualities enumerated in his words." "<b>אנשי חיל וגו׳.</b> He said four gradations corresponding to the four appointments mentioned in his words: corresponding to chiefs of thousands he said <b>אנשי חיל</b>; the word חיל proclaims the extremity of perfection of happiness and character traits and valor and totality of perfection, for so must the chief of a thousand be. And corresponding to chiefs of hundreds he said <b>יראי אלהים</b>, for in this particular there is not so great a need, except that one who fears Elohim is fit to be appointed chief of a hundred. And he was precise in saying <b>יראי אלהים</b> — meaning they must be those who fear the fear of punishment, for you must know that one who fears in the aspect of the Name הויה, since he holds in his mind that HaShem is merciful and gracious, may sometimes sin and rely on His mercy since He prolongs His anger and the like; but the fear of punishment makes the intelligent tremble, as you will find that all the great ones of the world fear the strictures of judgment even though they know their deeds are upright and correct. Go out and learn from the statement of our Sages, may their memory be for blessing (חגיגה ד,ב), who said concerning the matter of the mistress of a ghost — when Samuel came up and brought Moses our master, peace be upon him, with him; look there at their words. And therefore he said ״יראי אלהים״, for they must be in the category of fear of the strictures of judgment without mercy, and this aspect exists also in the chiefs of thousands, except that other particulars are added to them that are not needed for the chief of a hundred. And corresponding to chiefs of fifty he said <b>אנשי אמת</b> — this suffices for this category even if they have not attained this level of fear of the severity of judgment, but they must be men of truth who strive to uphold truth so it not be lacking. And corresponding to chiefs of tens he said <b>שונאי בצע</b> — this category suffices for them, and with this they will be fit to be chiefs of tens. And he was precise in saying <b>שונאי</b>, meaning, that the matter should be hateful to them — not that they need to distance their desire, for such people are suspect of being overcome by the enticing evil impulse. And all the traits of the three are found in אנשי חיל, and all the traits of the two are found in יראי אלהים, and the trait of שונאי בצע is found in אנשי אמת — and these are progressively higher, one above the other. And therefore he did not say ״ויראי״ etc. and ״אנשי אמת״ and ״שונאי בצע,״ for if all of them were to gather together in one person he should have said a vav in each one, or at least in the last division; but rather surely as I have written, that there is no need for this in all the particulars." "And behold, it is obvious that if he finds from the praiseworthy sort among all of them — for all the appointments — how good and how pleasant! And therefore when Moses sought in Israel, he found from the praiseworthy sort ״אנשי חיל״ — a measure sufficient for all of them, as it says ״ויבחר משה אנשי חיל מכל ישראל״, and there was no need to specify the other particulars since they are subsumed in the category of אנשי חיל. And this also justifies for you our interpretation of Jethro's words: that if his intention was to say this and more, then Moses should have chosen also all the qualities alluded to in Jethro's words. And one cannot say, Heaven forbid, that he did not find in Israel God-fearers, men of truth, etc.; but rather surely as we have said — that Jethro did not mention the other particulars except because Jethro was concerned lest he not find a sufficient measure at the highest level, and he was in doubt concerning a lesser rank as mentioned." "<b>וראיתי</b> to set the mind upon how Jethro merited that this passage be written through him. And it is true that he honored Moses the servant of HaShem, and behold his reward is that HaShem honored him; only HaShem could have done it for him in another way of honor — not in this manner, which appears, Heaven forbid, as a diminution of knowledge in the people of HaShem, until a priest of Midyan came and gave them counsel." "<b>And it will be seen</b> that the reason for the matter is to show HaShem to the children of Israel — that generation and every generation — that among the nations there are great ones in understanding and in wisdom. Go out and learn from the wisdom of Jethro in his counsel and the manner of the arrangement of men that he chose, for there are among the nations those who recognize matters of success. And the intention in this is that the election of Israel did not come about because they possess wisdom and discernment more than all the nations — and this is your sign: the wisdom of Jethro. From this you have learned that it was not because of the abundance of Israel's wisdom and their intellect that HaShem chose them, but rather because of supernal lovingkindness and because of the love of the fathers. And this reason will be sweeter to the palate to him who says (Zevaḥim 116a) that Jethro came before the giving of the Torah, for HaShem contrived this before the giving of the Torah, to declare that even though among the nations there are some wiser than Israel, none the less us has HaShem brought to Himself and chosen us. And upon this in particular we must give praise to Him who chose us from the side of His lovingkindness. Also according to him who says that he came after the giving of the Torah, there is a reason in that Scripture arranged his arrival before revealing the aforementioned intention, for without this the aforementioned intention would not be disclosed. And understand:"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :register {:flags {3 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:30:04.805784649Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}